<worksheet xml:id="special-expected-values">
    <title>Special Mathematical Expectations (2.3) Part 1</title>
    <page>
    <p>
        We'll start by recalling (or learning) some useful formulas.
    </p>
  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
        <p>
             The sum of the first <m>n</m> numbers
      <m>1 + 2 + 3 + \cdots + n = \displaystyle{\sum_{x=1}^n x}</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
        <p>
              One can check (use induction) that
      <m>1^{2} + 2^{2} + 3^{2} + \cdots + n^{2} = \displaystyle{\sum_{x=1}^n x^2}=</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="3in">
    <statement>
        <p>
             Find the sum: <m>S= a+ar+ar^{2}+ar^{3} + \cdots</m> (where <m>|r|\lt 1</m>).
    </p>
    <p>
      Start with a finite sum:
      <m>S_{n}= a + a\cdot r + a\cdot r^{2} + \cdots + a\cdot r^{n-1}</m>
    </p>
    </statement>
    </exercise>
     
</page>
<page>
     <definition xml:id="def-mean">
     <title>Mean</title>
     <statement>
        <p>
      Let <m>X</m> be a discrete random variable with space
      <m>S=\{x_{1},x_{2},...,x_{k},...\}</m> and with pmf <m>f(x)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      The <fillin characters="12"/> <m>X</m>, denoted <m>\mu_X</m>, or just <m>\mu</m> if the context is clear, is just the expected value of <m>X</m> 
      <md>
        E(X)=\mu=\sum_{x\in S}xf(x)=x_{1}f(x_{1})+x_{2}f(x_{2})+\cdots x_{k}f(x_{k})+\cdots.
      </md>
    </p>
     </statement>
     </definition>         
    

     <exercise workspace="0.5in">
     <definition xml:id="def-variance">
     <title>Variance</title>
     <statement>
        <p>
      Let <m>X</m> be a discrete random variable with space
      <m>S=\{x_{1},x_{2},...,x_{k},...\}</m> and with pmf <m>f(x)</m>.
    </p>
  <p>
      The <fillin characters="20"/> of <m>X</m>, denoted <m>\sigma^{2}</m> is the weighted mean
      of the square of the distance of each point in the space of <m>X</m> to the mean:
    </p>
     </statement>
     </definition>         
     </exercise>

    <definition xml:id="def-standard-deviation">
    <title>Standard Deviation</title>
    
    
        <statement>
            <p>
            
      Let <m>X</m> be a discrete random variable with space
      <m>S=\{x_{1},x_{2},...,x_{k},...\}</m> and with pmf <m>f(x)</m>. The positive square root of the variance is the
      <alert> standard deviation </alert> of <m>X</m> and is denoted by
      <m>\sigma</m>. </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

  <exercise workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
          <p>
      Note that because of the linear properties of expected value we get:
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>E[(X-\mu)^{2}]=</m>
    </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</page>
<page>
  <exercise workspace="3in">
    <statement>
        <p>
             Let <m>X</m> have uniform distribution on the space
      <m>\{1,2,3,4,5\}</m>. Find the mean and variance of <m>X</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

<exercise workspace ="5in">
    <statement>
        <p>
      More generally suppose that <m>X</m> has a uniform distribution on the
      first <m>m</m> positive integers. The mean of <m>X</m> is:
    </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>
</page>
<page>
   <exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
              Recall that <m>X</m> has a <em>hypergeometric distribution</em> in the
      situation where you are selecting <m>n</m> objects at random without
      replacement from a set of <m>N</m> objects composed of <m>N_{1}</m> of one
      type and <m>N_{2}</m> of another ( <m>N=N_{1}+N_{2})</m>. <m>X</m> is the
      number of objects of type <m>N_{1}</m>. 
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.25in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            We saw in the last section that <m>\mu=E[X]=</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    </task>
     <task workspace="0.25in">
    <statement>
        <p>
             It can be shown (see book) that <m>\sigma^{2} = Var(X) =</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
    </task>
    
   </exercise>

<exercise workspace="2in">
<statement>
    <p>
        Suppose you have a stack of <m>100</m> lottery tickets
      of which <m>15</m> are winners. You draw <m>5</m> at random. Let <m>X</m>
      be the number of winning tickets. Find <m>\mu, \sigma^{2}</m>.
    </p>
</statement>
</exercise>
</page>
<page>

    <p><alert>
      Practice
      </alert>
    </p>
    <exercise workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
        <p>
             Suppose <m>X</m> is a discrete RV with pmf given by
            <m>f(x)=\frac{4-x}{6}</m> for <m>x=1,2,3</m>. Find the mean and the
            variance of <m>X</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Suppose a fair, 12 sided die is rolled. Let <m>X</m> be the top
            face. Find the mean and Variance of <m>X</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            In a bowl of <q>fun size</q> candy, there are 15 Twix bars and 20
            Snickers. I reach in and grab 5 candies at random. Let <m>X</m> be
            the number of Twix. Find the mean and Variance of <m>X</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
    </exercise>
    </page>
     </worksheet>
